  designNotes: "Beat 5: fight upward \u2014 the deck garrison is the target and the win is units dead.
    This is a POWER beat, not an angle beat, and that is MEASURED (2026-09-02), not assumed.
    NO PLAYER TANK (2026-08-21): the three shells were the structure-killer (96x3=288) and
    turned the loft into a 3-shell errand. Crew folded into the ground line (2+5 -> 7). HP
    not retuned yet \u2014 win is units dead, so the loft onto the platform is the beat; if
    the tower then reads as a wall, cut hpScale before inventing a shop. Bunker moved 1.8 ->
    5.0; at 11.3 the separation was the worst in the campaign. Rule 7 used to warn because
    the tank crew (back rank) could not reach the platform; without them the warning may
    clear. Structure hp RETUNED 2026-08-07: bunker hpScale 0.55 (250 -> 137). Tower garrison
    2026-08-21: those were MACHINE GUNNERS (6 crowd), not snipers. MG belong in the street
    (3, at 3.5). ONE sniper on the platform \u2014 elevated, back, hard to reach. The loft
    is still the beat; the payload is one 16-hp gun, not a roof of burst fire. PLAYS HARD
    \u2014 2026-08-25, Rob: 'group player units together more closely, add a few more player
    units.' Squad 10 -> 13 (riflemen 7 -> 10) and deployBudget 13 -> 16. Line packed to
    playerSpacingScale 0.8. Race 0.7x -> 0.4x. Device 08-25: bunker down T6, 13 standing,
    ZERO casualties. OVERSHOT. Walked back 2026-08-27: riflemen 10 -> 7 (squad 13 -> 10),
    deployBudget 16 -> 13, packing KEPT at 0.8. The grouping was the ask that read; the
    extra three bodies were the wipe. Sniper pulled to the LEDGE, anchorX 10 -> 9.4 (deck
    spans 9.25..10.75), and given flatTrajectory so it fires a direct 12-20 degree shot
    instead of a 35-60 lob. The 11.6 separation warning is OLDER than all of this and
    unchanged by it. ANGLE CLAIM RETIRED 2026-09-02. This file used to open 'the first level
    where the ANGLE matters more than the power'. The 09-01 play won it in twelve volleys at
    a FIXED 45 degrees, varying power alone (78 -> 82 -> 67 -> 86 -> 88), so the claim was
    swept: at the recorded muzzle x of -8.6, EVERY angle from 25 to 65 degrees kills the
    deck garrison, each with its own power band about 7 points of 100 wide. A 40-degree-wide
    legal window is not a skill axis; a 7-point power band is. What the level actually
    teaches is that band \u2014 78% grazes the bunker face for 24 chip damage and 82% clears
    onto the deck for six dead, four points between nothing and the level. The cause is
    geometric, not tuning: a round clears a structure's leading edge at tan(angle) x
    half-hitWidth, and the bunker's half-width (1.25) already exceeds its deck height
    (1.125), so 45 degrees clears the face at any power. FORCING a loft would need deck
    height greater than half-width \u2014 do not attempt it here without an ask; the 2-star
    balance was walked back twice to land. PRODUCT_DIRECTION's beat chart asks L5 for
    elevation / deck fight, never for angle, and that is what it delivers. Sniper footnote
    from the same sweep: the tower rider IS directly hittable, but only in a 2-3 point
    needle (95-97% at 45 degrees) because the platform box shadows him \u2014 in practice he
    is a COLLAPSE target, which is what the goal text already says."
